re that the Fidelity of the  actor's performance wasn't lost by going  digital they would be able to take the  nuance and subtleties of Dan Stevens  performance and reflect them in the  Beast himself according to McCulloch if  you were trying to do it under a  thousand pounds of makeup it was never  going to give us the clarity of  performance at digital domain ultimately  achieved in beast when Disney decided to  remake 1977's Pete's Dragon it turned to  a bender with a proven track record of  bringing fantasy creatures to life wet  at digital WETA has created some of the  most memorable CGI characters in movie  history and has worked with motion  capture Legend Andy Circus on multiple  occasions circus won praised for his  portrayal of evolved chimpanzee Caesar  in the Planet of the Apes reboot series  and what it took inspiration from that  character when it came to making Pete's  titular Dragon Elliot as well as Eric  Sandman told The Hollywood Reporter with  Caesar you really feel his pain and  happiness and with Elliot it's the same  the Big Challenge was getting the  emotion out of Elliott the connection  between the dragon and Pete is such a  big part of the film unlike Caesar  however Elliot wasn't portrayed by an  actor in a motion capture suit sand and  recalled we had a green wire mesh head  that I would lift over my head I tried  to move the head as best I thought it  would work for the shot and then we  track the head with our CG Elliot  Peter Jackson's King Kong won three  Academy Awards in 2006. one of which was  for wedded digital's groundbreaking pfx  work and the famous ape still looks  incredible today but the company became  even better at rendering furry animals  in the decade that followed in an  interview with The Hollywood Reporter  why does Eric Sanden said King Kong had  about 1 million hairs on him and  probably one quarter of those hairs were  simulated and we interpolated the rest  on Elliott we had 20 million and we  individually simulated each hair so when  Pete is petting or interacting with him  we get the interaction with the hair  according to weta's head of creatures  getting Elliot's fur to look real during  close-up interactions was definitely  tricky but The Airborne sequences proved  most challenging GEOS Johnson told  cartoon Brew there are lots of flying  shots in the movie and dialing in the  right amount of wind for a dragonflying  at very fast speeds also took some doing  fans reacted with a mixture of  bemusement and horror when the first  trailer for Disney's live-action Aladdin  drop revealing Will Smith's genie in all  his blueness for the first time  certainly the former Fresh Prince of  Bel-Air star knew he had some extremely  big shoes to fill but so did the visual  effects artist tasked with turning him  into a Disney icon the remakes VFX  supervisor Chaz Jarrett told CBR we  always sort of knew it was going to be  contentious I suppose that there would  be comparisons between our Genie and the  original But ultimately we felt  competent in the character we created  finalizing the Genie's look wasn't easy  either the team had been working on the  character for a year and a half by the  time the first trailer came out  constantly tinkering with his appearance  according to Jarrett he wasn't initially  supposed to look much like Smith at all  and the VFX team experimented with a  range of more cartoonish blue Genies  during pre-production ultimately however  they decided that they wouldn't be  making the best use of the film's  biggest star with this approach as Jared  says when you've got Will Smith you  realize what makes Will Smith work as  all the little Will Smith mannerisms if  you try putting those mannerisms on  something that isn't Will Smith it isn't  quite satisfied  according to Aladdin's visual effects  supervisor the iconic A Whole New World  scene would have played out quite  differently had it not been for director  guy Ritchie's Vision which asked Jarrett  and his team first started working on  the Magic Carpet Ride they envisioned  the journey taking place over the course  of an entire day but the titular Street  rat picking up the princess at Sunrise  and dropping her back off at the palace  right after Sunset Jared told Art of bfx  it worked well but guy felt strongly  that he wanted it all to happen at night  as in the original so he took helicopter  units to a number of locations to Jordan  and Namibia and Africa and filmed  elements the footage they captured  Blended well with the footage of mina  Masood and Naomi Scott who mind their  own recording of the song in front of a  giant blue screen it literally felt like  you know a Magic Carpet it was it was  incredible according to Naomi Scott the  scene was quite the workout too since  the actors had to support their own  weight as the rig shifted from side to  side  the dress transformation scene isn't  just one of the most iconic moments of  the original animated Cinderella and  it's one of the most iconic Disney  moments period naturally the studio used  Snippets from the scene to promote its  live action remake but this move ended  up backfiring big time in one trailer  for 2015 Cinderella star Lily James  appears to look a lot thinner around the  waist than she normally does and the  pre-bfx footage seemed to back up claims  that her waistline had been digitally  altered the British actress denied that  this was the case during an interview  with the Los Angeles Times insisting  that she was just a really healthy  person with a naturally small waist the  actress went on to complain about people  who were in her opinion kicking up a big  bus over nothing she said I think all  that stuff's so negative and you've got  to let it wash over your head but how  does she explain images that seem to  prove that her waist miraculously  changed sizes it's all down to her onset  Fairy Godmother costume designer Sandy  Powell James explained the skirts big  and the corset pulls me in and that's  the point that's the shape Sandy Powell  created  Tim Burton's remake of 1941's Dumbo was  one of the most ambitious Disney Live  Action projects from a visual effects  standpoint largely because of the  eponymous elephant Dumbo was animated  entirely from scratch in certain scenes  but when the blue eyed elephant needed  to interact with one of the humans in  the story creature Actor Ed Osmond came  in Osman was kitted out with crutch-like  arm extensions and had to walk around on  all fours for two to three hours per day  wearing a variety of different green  suits the actor told Insider it's a very  strange job the struggle with the arm  extensions is if you don't get the  Rhythm right if you don't do right arm  left leg left arm right leg you end up  walking like a table Osman was also  hooked up to an earpiece so he could  communicate with the film's VFX  supervisor NPC's Richard stammers during  his scenes according to stammers the 4  foot 6 actor was crucial to facilitating  physical interaction and eye lines  during some of the remake's most pivotal  moments stammers told Art of bfx having  Ed there allowed Tim to direct the  performance and we would always do our  first rehearsal and takes with that in  place  the hardest part of remaking Dumbo was  always going to be the flying scenes  it's easy to suspend your sense of  disbelief for animated movies but when  you're watching a live action film and  the animals look real that becomes a lot  harder to do in order to make the flight  scenes feel as genuine as possible the  VFX team did a lot of research into  birds and how their wings work in  relation to their body NPC's animation  supervisor Catherine Mullen told  animation World Network you're trying to  get as much anticipation and effort into  each of the two or three giant flaps  when Dumbo gets off the ground those  were the key moments also making sure  that he isn't rising up too quickly or  easily Dumbo flying solo was a challenge  but things got even more complicated  when the elephant had Riders a six-axis  rig was constructed and the VFX team  used what MPC supervisor Richard  stammers described as visual production  techniques in a nutshell director Tim  Burton could see a crude version of what  Dumbo would actually look like in the  finished scene there and then stammers  explains Ava Green and the children Nico  Parker and Finley hobbins got to ride  this rig with each wide or Midtown not  specially programmed onset compositing  enabled Tim to approve performances in  context to the animation within minutes  of each take being captured Winnie the  Pooh and the rest of the Hundred Acre  Wood gang were painstakingly brought to  life in 2018's live-action Christopher  Robin but bringing the honey-loving bear  and his friends out of the woods and  into England's capital city was a  mammoth task for the visual effects crew  crane store's VFX supervisor Chris  Lawrence singled out some key members of  his team during an in-depth interview  with shoot online including the vendor's  Global director of animation Michael  Ames Lawrence explained Ames brought an  incredible attention to detail and the  valuable knowledge of a practitioner  often he would simply sketch exactly  what he wanted I thought with Winnie the  Pooh he achieved one of the most subtle  nuanced animated screen performances  ever seen no this is very confusing it  may be the hunger you've just eaten oh  that's right  maybe I didn't need enough bringing  Winnie the Pooh to the real world meant  that actor Ewan McGregor had to act  opposite some pretty creepy headless  dolls in some scenes but this was  nothing new for the Scottish star who  told Screen Rant I did it all in the  Star Wars movies quite a lot it's like a  sort of skill just something else that  you have to learn to do  there are a lot of weird and wonderful  characters in Alice in Wonderland and  bringing each one to life in live action  posed a unique challe